# Coordinate Geometry: Algebra Meets Geometry

> Every point on the plane can be named by a pair of numbers, and every
> geometric question — distance, midpoint, slope, area — then becomes an
> algebra calculation. This lesson builds that toolkit step by step.

## 1. The Cartesian Plane

Two perpendicular number lines — the x-axis and the y-axis — cross at
the **origin** O(0, 0) and divide the plane into four **quadrants**.
A point P is written as an ordered pair $(x, y)$: $x$ is the
**abscissa** (horizontal distance from the origin) and $y$ the
**ordinate** (vertical distance).

| Quadrant | Sign of x | Sign of y | Example point |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| I | positive | positive | (3, 2) |
| II | negative | positive | (−3, 2) |
| III | negative | negative | (−3, −2) |
| IV | positive | negative | (3, −2) |

[definition]
The **Cartesian coordinate system** assigns each point of the plane a
unique ordered pair of real numbers, and each ordered pair a unique
point — a perfect two-way dictionary between geometry and algebra.
[/definition]

Points on the axes belong to no quadrant: (5, 0) sits on the x-axis
and (0, −4) on the y-axis.

## 2. The Distance Formula

Distance is Pythagoras' theorem in disguise. Between $A(x_1, y_1)$ and
$B(x_2, y_2)$ the horizontal leg is $x_2 - x_1$ and the vertical leg
is $y_2 - y_1$, so the hypotenuse AB satisfies:

@math label="eq:distance"
d = \sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2}
@/math

[example]
Find the distance between A(−2, 3) and B(4, −5).
Step 1: horizontal leg = 4 − (−2) = 6.
Step 2: vertical leg = −5 − 3 = −8.
Step 3: $d = \sqrt{6^2 + (-8)^2} = \sqrt{100} = 10$ units.
[/example]

[tip]
The formula squares each difference, so signs never matter:
(−8)² and 8² both give 64. Just square the two legs and add — then
take the square root last.
[/tip]

## 3. Midpoint and Section Formulas

The midpoint is the coordinate-wise average of the endpoints:

@math label="eq:midpoint"
M = \left( \frac{x_1+x_2}{2}, \frac{y_1+y_2}{2} \right)
@/math

For example, the midpoint of (−3, 5) and (7, −1) is
((−3 + 7)/2, (5 + (−1))/2) = (2, 2).

When a point P divides the segment from A to B internally in the ratio
m : n (measured from A), it sits closer to B as m grows:

@math label="eq:section"
P = \left( \frac{m x_2 + n x_1}{m+n}, \frac{m y_2 + n y_1}{m+n} \right)
@/math

[example]
Find the point dividing A(2, 3) to B(8, 12) in the ratio 2 : 1.
Step 1: x = (2 × 8 + 1 × 2)/3 = 18/3 = 6.
Step 2: y = (2 × 12 + 1 × 3)/3 = 27/3 = 9, so P(6, 9).
Check: from A to B the run is 6 and the rise is 9; P has covered
2/3 of both — exactly the ratio 2 : 1.
[/example]

The **centroid** of a triangle extends the same averaging idea:
average the three vertices' coordinates separately.

## 4. Slope: The Steepness of a Line

@math label="eq:slope"
m = \frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} = \tan\theta
@/math

| Value of m | The line |
| --- | --- |
| m > 0 | rises from left to right |
| m < 0 | falls from left to right |
| m = 0 | horizontal |
| undefined | vertical (horizontal step is zero) |

[example]
Slope through (2, 1) and (6, 9): m = (9 − 1)/(6 − 2) = 8/4 = 2.
The line climbs 2 units for every 1 across, matching tan θ = 2,
so it makes an angle of about 63.4° with the positive x-axis.
[/example]

[warning]
A vertical line has no slope at all — dividing by the zero horizontal
step is impossible. "Undefined slope" and "zero slope" are opposite
situations: vertical versus horizontal.
[/warning]

## 5. The Five Forms of a Line's Equation

| Form | Equation | Best for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Point-slope | y − y₁ = m(x − x₁) | one known point and the slope |
| Slope-intercept | y = mx + c | reading slope and y-intercept |
| Two-point | line through (x₁, y₁), (x₂, y₂) | two known points |
| Intercept form | x/a + y/b = 1 | axis intercepts a and b |
| General form | ax + by + c = 0 | uniform standard for computation |

@diagram tree id="line-form-family" layout="tree" aria="Family tree of the five standard forms of a line's equation"
  "Equation of a line" -> "Point-slope: y - y1 = m(x - x1)"
  "Equation of a line" -> "Slope-intercept: y = mx + c"
  "Equation of a line" -> "Two-point form"
  "Equation of a line" -> "Intercept form: x/a + y/b = 1"
  "Equation of a line" -> "General form: ax + by + c = 0"
@/diagram

[note]
Every form converts to every other. In the general form ax + by + c = 0
the slope is m = −a/b and the intercepts are x = −c/a and y = −c/b, so
a conversion is never more than one rearrangement away.
[/note]

## 6. Parallel and Perpendicular Lines

@math label="eq:parallel-perpendicular"
\text{Parallel: } m_1 = m_2 \qquad \text{Perpendicular: } m_1 m_2 = -1
@/math

[example]
Find the line through (2, −1) perpendicular to y = 3x + 4.
Step 1: the given slope is 3, so the perpendicular slope is −1/3.
Step 2: point-slope gives y + 1 = −(1/3)(x − 2).
Step 3: rearrange to x + 3y + 1 = 0.
Check: the slopes 3 and −1/3 multiply to −1. Perpendicular confirmed.
[/example]

[tip]
To test whether two lines are perpendicular you only need the product
of their slopes: −1 means yes, anything else means no. Equal slopes
mean parallel — and equal slopes with a shared point mean the same line.
[/tip]

## 7. Area of a Triangle from Coordinates

@math label="eq:triangle-area"
A = \frac{1}{2} \left| x_1(y_2-y_3) + x_2(y_3-y_1) + x_3(y_1-y_2) \right|
@/math

[example]
Vertices (0, 0), (4, 0), (4, 3):
A = ½ |0(0 − 3) + 4(3 − 0) + 4(0 − 0)| = ½ |12| = 6 square units —
the same answer the base-height formula gives (½ × 4 × 3).
[/example]

If the formula returns 0, the three points are **collinear** — no
triangle exists. That zero-area test is the standard coordinate proof
of collinearity.

## 8. Putting It All Together

[example]
Classify the triangle A(1, 2), B(4, 6), C(7, 2).
Step 1 (sides): AB = √(9 + 16) = 5, BC = √(9 + 16) = 5, AC = 6.
Two equal sides, so the triangle is isosceles.
Step 2 (area): ½ |1(6 − 2) + 4(2 − 2) + 7(2 − 6)| = ½ |−24| = 12.
Cross-check: base AC = 6 along y = 2, height = 4, area = 12. Agreed.
[/example]

Plotting y = 2x + 1 — slope 2, y-intercept 1 — shows how a straight
line rises steadily through its points:

@chart line title="y = 2x + 1" labels:
0
1
2
3
4

data:
1
3
5
7
9
@/chart

[info]
Coordinate geometry is the language behind GPS navigation, computer
graphics, and robotics: every "distance to destination", collision
test, or rendered triangle is one of these formulas running on
coordinates.
[/info]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Tool | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Distance A(−2, 3) to B(4, −5) | distance formula | 10 |
| Midpoint of (−3, 5), (7, −1) | average coordinates | (2, 2) |
| Point 2 : 1 from A(2, 3) to B(8, 12) | section formula | (6, 9) |
| Slope through (2, 1), (6, 9) | rise over run | 2 |
| Line perpendicular to y = 3x + 4 through (2, −1) | m × m = −1 | x + 3y + 1 = 0 |
| Area of (0, 0), (4, 0), (4, 3) | shoelace formula | 6 |
| Triangle (1, 2), (4, 6), (7, 2) | side lengths | isosceles, area 12 |

[summary]
The plane turns into arithmetic: distances come from Pythagoras,
midpoints from averaging, slopes from rise over run, parallel lines
from equal slopes, perpendicular lines from slopes multiplying to −1,
and areas from the shoelace formula — with a zero area proving
collinearity.
[/summary]
